# Keys permitted to approve a landing.
#
# 'just land' refuses a branch tip that is not signed by one of these. Every key
# here must be hardware-backed (sk-ssh-*): a FIDO2 signature cannot be produced
# without someone physically touching the authenticator, which is the entire
# point. A software key here would silently reduce "a human approved this" to
# "this machine produced it".
#
# This file is committed so that changing who may approve is a reviewable diff
# rather than somebody's local git config.
#
# The comment on the key below carries no device serial. ssh-keygen puts one in
# the comment it generates; it was taken out when the key was added here. That
# was tidiness, not secrecy -- a serial cannot produce a signature, it is printed
# on the authenticator, and Yubico's attestation certificates contain it.
#
# The serial is still in this file's git history. That is a decision, not an
# oversight: see bead gc-24y. Taking it out means rewriting every commit from
# f9134d4 onward, which invalidates the hardware signatures this file exists to
# demand, and GitHub keeps the old blobs on refs/pull/* either way. The audit in
# that bead also confirms what is *not* in history -- no tokens, no endpoints, no
# bead prose. Read it before proposing the rewrite.
